  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 156 PM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nThe upper-level trough that will help bring precipitation across the \narea today into Saturday will finally depart the region to the \neast Sunday, leading to a period of dry conditions. Surface \nhigh pressure building across the area, along with upper-level \nridging over the Intermountain West region protruding from a \nlarge upper-level high over the southwestern CONUS, will support\nmore quiescent conditions through Tuesday, with near-seasonal \ntemperatures (highs in the lower 80s) for Sunday through \nTuesday.\n\nIn terms of active weather, it appears mainly Wednesday/Thursday \nwill be the time to watch as the upper-level ridging breaks down, \nallowing a mid-level trough to approach the region. An attendant \nsurface cold front is also progged to accompany the trough, although \nthere are significant timing differences among the global models of \nthe front, and thus, timing of precipitation with that boundary. \nSevere weather potential with this front appears to be pretty low at \nthis time, given modest values of instability and shear (25-35 knots \nfor 0-6 km shear magnitudes). The various machine learning severe \nweather probability guidance supports the more limited severe \nweather signal.\n\nSchultz",
